<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In-context learning (ICL) is a key building block of modern large language models, yet its theoretical mechanisms remain poorly understood.
It is particularly mysterious how ICL operates in real-world applications where tasks have a common structure.
In this work, we address this problem by analyzing a linear attention model trained on low-rank regression tasks. Within this setting, we precisely characterize the distribution of predictions and the generalization error in the high-dimensional limit.
Moreover, we find that statistical fluctuations in finite pre-training data induce an implicit regularization.
Finally, we identify a sharp phase transition of the generalization error governed by task structure.
These results provide a framework for understanding how transformers learn to learn the task structure.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A defining characteristic of modern large-scale language models (LLMs) is their capacity for in-context learning (ICL).
This allows them to perform new tasks by conditioning on a few examples provided in context, without requiring any parameter updates.
This capability has led to impressive performance on a wide array of tasks, including few-shot classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>]</cite>, complex reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2024</a>]</cite> and code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Chen, 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong and Wang, 2024</a>]</cite>.
The surprising effectiveness of ICL has naturally attracted significant theoretical interest in uncovering underlying principles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, most fundamental studies of ICL have focused on settings with independent tasks.
In practice, tasks are often not independent but share underlying structures, as widely recognized in multi-task learning (MTL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caruana, 1997</a>]</cite>.
In this setting, assuming a shared low-rank structure among tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han and Zhang, 2016</a>]</cite> has led to effective algorithms across domains such as computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>]</cite>, natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2005</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ando and Zhang, 2005</a>]</cite>, and bioinformatics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pong et al., 2010</a>]</cite>.
This suggests that it is equally important to examine how ICL behaves when tasks share such common structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another limitation of current ICL theory is its lack of precise analysis and interpretability.
On the one hand, existing studies can predict overall performance and reproduce key phenomena such as double descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>]</cite> and emergence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raventós et al., 2023</a>]</cite> in toy models, but these studies offer limited interpretability.
On the other hand, prior works have shown that transformers <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">can</span> implement useful algorithms for regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al., 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>]</cite>, yet the precise mathematical form of the predictors they <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actually</span> acquire through training has remained elusive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we aim to gain insight into these issues by analyzing an attention-only transformer trained on linear regression tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2022</a>]</cite> with a low-rank structure.
Although the model is simple, it exhibits rich and nontrivial phenomena. It has therefore been extensively studied and shown to be instrumental in explaining various aspects of ICL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al., 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samet and Zafer, 2025</a>]</cite> both theoretically and empirically.
To interpret the mechanisms of ICL effectively, we situate our analysis in the high-dimensional asymptotic regime, where the system’s complex behavior can be precisely characterized by a small number of macroscopic order parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zavatone-Veth et al., 2025</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">At the core of our analysis is a precise solution of a linear attention model trained on regression tasks in the high-dimensional limit.
This framework reveals a decomposition of the ICL prediction into two parts:
an algorithmic component that performs linear regression,
and a noise component whose effect depends on the information acquired during pre-training (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
When the tasks share a low-rank structure, additional phenomena emerge.
First, the model learns an efficient algorithm that exploits the low-rank structure of the tasks.
Moreover, when the prompt contains memorized tasks or the learned task structure, the noise component can be suppressed (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
Second, the learning of low-rank tasks is stabilized by an implicit regularization induced by finite-data statistics during pre-training (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Finally, we show that the rank of the pre-training tasks induces a sharp phase transition in the model’s capabilities, from a regime where the model cannot fully exploit the low-rank structure to one where the low-rank structure is fully exploited.
In the latter regime, the model faces a fundamental trade-off between specialization to pre-trained (in-distribution) tasks and robustness to unseen out-of-distribution tasks (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Trade-offs from Task Difficulty" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The code is available in <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/icl-replica-analysis-3854" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/icl-replica-analysis-3854</a>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Model</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the mechanism of ICL using a transformer-based architecture.
The overall goal is to train a model that can infer and execute the algorithm for linear regression purely from a sequence of examples provided in its context.
To this end, we formalize a problem setup consisting of a pre-training phase, where the model learns the general algorithm,
and an inference phase, where its ability to generalize to new instances is evaluated<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          A comprehensive list of notations is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Summary of Notations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for reference.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Pretraining Phase</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by constructing a base collection of tasks <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{0}=\{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid\mu=1,\ldots,M_{0}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{0}=\{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}\mid\mu=1,\ldots,M_{0}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents a diverse pool of possible task vectors. Each <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated from a low-dimensional latent structure: we fix a shared feature matrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> with orthonormal columns (<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq D</annotation></semantics></math>), draw a latent task feature vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}^{\mu}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},I_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}^{\mu}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},I_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>, and map it to the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space via <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{\mu}=\sqrt{D/r}A\mathbf{v}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{\mu}=\sqrt{D/r}A\mathbf{v}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">From this base collection, we then form the actual training set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> by sampling <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(\gg M_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≫</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\gg M_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> tasks uniformly with replacement. This step reflects the fact that, in the thermodynamic limit, obtaining nontrivial learning behavior requires that individual tasks may recur many times. In other words, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the diversity of available tasks, while <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the effective workload that the learner repeatedly encounters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each task <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{\mu}\in\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{\mu}\in\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, we create a training instance
by first drawing <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L+1</annotation></semantics></math> input vectors, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\mu}\}_{i=1}^{L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\mu}\}_{i=1}^{L+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
independently from a normal distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{D}/D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{D}/D)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding outputs are then generated as <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{\mu}=\mathbf{w}^{\mu}\cdot\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\mu}+\epsilon_{i}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{\mu}=\mathbf{w}^{\mu}\cdot\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\mu}+\epsilon_{i}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where each <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{i}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{i}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent noise term drawn from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\mathbf{x}_{l}^{\mu},y_{l}^{\mu})\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\mathbf{x}_{l}^{\mu},y_{l}^{\mu})\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, serve as <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">demonstrations</span>,
while the final pair, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x}_{L+1}^{\mu},y_{L+1}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x}_{L+1}^{\mu},y_{L+1}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>, constitutes the <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">query</span> and its <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">label</span>.
The entire generative process for these instances is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pretrain}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pretrain</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pretrain}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These elements are concatenated to form the input sequence for the model, which we refer to as the <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">context</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mu}=\ab(\matrixquantity{\lx@physics@matrix\vb{x}_1^\mu&amp; \vb{x}_2^\mu&amp; \cdots&amp; \vb{x}_L^\mu&amp; \vb{x}_{L+1}^\mu\\
y_1^\mu&amp; y_2^\mu&amp; \cdots&amp; y_L^\mu&amp; 0\endlx@physics@matrix})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mu}=\ab(\matrixquantity{\lx@physics@matrix\vb{x}_1^\mu&amp; \vb{x}_2^\mu&amp; \cdots&amp; \vb{x}_L^\mu&amp; \vb{x}_{L+1}^\mu\\
y_1^\mu&amp; y_2^\mu&amp; \cdots&amp; y_L^\mu&amp; 0\endlx@physics@matrix}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the label corresponding to the query <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{L+1}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{L+1}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced with a placeholder value of zero, indicating that it is the unknown quantity the model is required to predict.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our model is an attention-based architecture that maps an input context to a prediction. This is expressed as the composition of an attention function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta}:\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}\to\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝖾𝗇</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta}:\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}\to\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, and a readout function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Read}:\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Read}:\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The prediction for the query is given by:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{L+1}^{\mu}=\mathsf{Read}(\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta}(C^{\mu}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝖾𝗇</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{L+1}^{\mu}=\mathsf{Read}(\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta}(C^{\mu})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The model is trained by minimizing the mean squared error (MSE) loss between the predictions and the true labels over all <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> training instances. The loss function is:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\Theta)=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}(\hat{y}_{L+1}^{\mu}-y_{L+1}^{\mu})^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\Theta)=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}(\hat{y}_{L+1}^{\mu}-y_{L+1}^{\mu})^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Through optimization, we obtain the learned parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{*}=\arg\min_{\Theta}\mathcal{L}(\Theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{*}=\arg\min_{\Theta}\mathcal{L}(\Theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Inference Phase</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the inference phase, we evaluate the generalization ability of the pre-trained model
with fixed optimal parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Performance is assessed on new test instances generated from a fixed evaluation task <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each test instance is created by first drawing <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}+1</annotation></semantics></math> input vectors, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{\tilde{L}+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{\tilde{L}+1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
independently from <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{D}/D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}_{D}/D)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}\leq L</annotation></semantics></math> reflects a few-shot learning scenario.
The corresponding outputs are generated without noise as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}=\mathbf{w}^{*}\cdot\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}=\mathbf{w}^{*}\cdot\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
to assess the model’s pure algorithmic reasoning.
The first <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs serve as the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">demonstrations</span>,
while the final pair serves as the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">query</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{\tilde{L}+1}=\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{\tilde{L}+1}=\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and its <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">label</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\tilde{L}+1}=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\tilde{L}+1}=y</annotation></semantics></math>.
We refer to this test data distribution for a given task <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the length of demonstrations <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}</annotation></semantics></math> may be less than <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the input sequence, referred to as the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prompt</span> <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, is padded with zero vectors to match the context length of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L+1</annotation></semantics></math> expected by the model.
The prompt <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(L+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed as:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\ab(\matrixquantity{\lx@physics@matrix\vb{x}_1 &amp; \cdots&amp; \vb{x}_{\tilde{L}} &amp; \vb{0} &amp; \cdots&amp; \vb{0} &amp; \vb{x} \\
y_1 &amp; \cdots&amp; y_{\tilde{L}} &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots&amp; 0 &amp; 0\endlx@physics@matrix})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mtd><mtd><mi>𝟎</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>𝟎</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>𝐱</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\ab(\matrixquantity{\lx@physics@matrix\vb{x}_1 &amp; \cdots&amp; \vb{x}_{\tilde{L}} &amp; \vb{0} &amp; \cdots&amp; \vb{0} &amp; \vb{x} \\
y_1 &amp; \cdots&amp; y_{\tilde{L}} &amp; 0 &amp; \cdots&amp; 0 &amp; 0\endlx@physics@matrix}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the final column is the query input <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with its label hidden.
The prediction <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is compared with the true label <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.
The model’s prediction for the query is obtained by applying the frozen network; <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\mathsf{Read}(\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta^{*}}(P))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝖾𝗇</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\mathsf{Read}(\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta^{*}}(P))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Evaluation Protocol</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the model’s ICL capabilities across three distinct protocols.</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Memorization (TM):</span> The task is sampled from the pre-training set, <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{*}\sim\mathsf{Unif}(\mathcal{W}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{*}\sim\mathsf{Unif}(\mathcal{W}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. This protocol tests the model’s ability to recall and execute a task seen during training, given a new set of demonstrations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-Distribution Generalization (IDG):</span> The task is novel but generated from the same underlying structure, <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{*}=\sqrt{D/r}A\mathbf{v}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{*}=\sqrt{D/r}A\mathbf{v}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, for a new latent vector <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}^{*}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},I_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}^{*}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},I_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>. This protocol measures the ability to generalize to unseen tasks that share the same low-dimensional structure as the training tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Out-of-Distribution Generalization (ODG):</span> The task is drawn from a standard isotropic Gaussian distribution, <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{*}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},I_{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝟎</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{*}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},I_{D})</annotation></semantics></math>. These tasks, by design, lack the low-rank structure inherent in the training distribution, thereby testing the model’s robustness to a fundamental structural mismatch.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The model’s performance on each protocol is quantified by its specific MSE, which we denote generically as <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{protocol}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>protocol</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{protocol}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{protocol}\in\{\text{TM, IDG, ODG}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>protocol</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>TM, IDG, ODG</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{protocol}\in\{\text{TM, IDG, ODG}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This error is formally defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{protocol}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{w}^{*}\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{protocol}}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{pretrain}}}\ab[(y-\hat{y})^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>protocol</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>protocol</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>test</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pretrain</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{protocol}}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{w}^{*}\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{protocol}}}\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}_{\text{test}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{pretrain}}}\ab[(y-\hat{y})^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{protocol}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>protocol</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{protocol}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the task distribution corresponding to each protocol as defined above.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Attention Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For analytical tractability, we now specify the general architecture to a single-layer linear attention model.
In this simplified setting, the attention mechanism is defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta}(C)=C+\frac{1}{L}VC(KC)^{\top}(QC)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖠𝗍𝗍𝖾𝗇</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Atten}_{\Theta}(C)=C+\frac{1}{L}VC(KC)^{\top}(QC),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the learnable parameters are <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta=\{V,K,Q\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta=\{V,K,Q\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are matrices in <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(D+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{(D+1)\times(D+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The readout function simply extracts the final scalar output at the query position: <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Read}(C^{\prime})=C^{\prime}_{D+1,L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖱𝖾𝖺𝖽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Read}(C^{\prime})=C^{\prime}_{D+1,L+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under this setting and certain simplifying assumptions, it is known that the pre-training process is equivalent to training a one-layer neural network (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>]</cite> and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Justification of Equivalence of the Linear Attention Model to a Linear Neural Network" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).
Specifically, the input context <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped to an effective feature matrix <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\mu}\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> for each training instance <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\mu}=\frac{D}{L}\ab[\mathbf{x}^{\mu}_{L+1}\sum_{l\leq L}y_{l}^{\mu}(\mathbf{x}^{\mu}_{l})^{\top}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\mu}=\frac{D}{L}\ab[\mathbf{x}^{\mu}_{L+1}\sum_{l\leq L}y_{l}^{\mu}(\mathbf{x}^{\mu}_{l})^{\top}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The attention model’s prediction is then equivalent to the output of a neural network, which is a linear function of this effective feature:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{L+1}^{\mu}=\mathrm{Tr}\ab(W{H^{\mu}}^{\top})=\sum_{i,j=1}^{D}W_{ij}H_{ij}^{\mu}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>H</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{L+1}^{\mu}=\mathrm{Tr}\ab(W{H^{\mu}}^{\top})=\sum_{i,j=1}^{D}W_{ij}H_{ij}^{\mu},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the trainable weights of the attention mechanism <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V,K,Q\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V,K,Q\}</annotation></semantics></math> are implicitly mapped to a single effective weight matrix <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{D\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This equivalence extends directly to the inference phase. For a given prompt <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrations, the effective feature matrix <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding prediction <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by:</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\frac{D}{\tilde{L}}\ab[\mathbf{x}\sum_{l\leq\tilde{L}}y_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{l})^{\top}]\quad\hat{y}=\mathrm{Tr}\ab(W^{*}H^{\top})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Tr</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\frac{D}{\tilde{L}}\ab[\mathbf{x}\sum_{l\leq\tilde{L}}y_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{l})^{\top}]\quad\hat{y}=\mathrm{Tr}\ab(W^{*}H^{\top}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the learned weight matrix from the pre-training phase.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>High-Dimensional Analysis</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the model’s typical performance, we consider the high-dimensional limit where the problem dimensions (<math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,L,\tilde{L},M_{0},M,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,L,\tilde{L},M_{0},M,r</annotation></semantics></math>) grow to infinity. The system’s behavior in this regime is characterized by several dimensionless parameters, which are assumed to be fixed constants in <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pre-training Sample Ratio</span> as <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=L/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=L/D</annotation></semantics></math> and the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference Sample Ratio</span> as <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}=\tilde{L}/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}=\tilde{L}/D</annotation></semantics></math>, which represent the ratio of examples to features during pre-training and at inference, respectively, with the constraint that <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}\leq\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}\leq\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we introduce the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Difficulty</span> <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=r/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=r/D</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the relative dimension of the latent task space where <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we define the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Diversity</span> as <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=M_{0}/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=M_{0}/D</annotation></semantics></math> to capture the richness of the base task set, and the <span id="S2.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Data Density</span> as <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=M/(DM_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=M/(DM_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> to represent the number of training instances per task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To characterize the model’s behavior, we define two regimes based on data density and task diversity.
For data density, we distinguish the data-rich regime (<math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) and the data-deficient regime (<math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\gamma&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\gamma&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>).
Independently, for task diversity, we distinguish the <span id="S2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">task-rich regime</span> (<math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), where the variety of tasks spans the latent space, and the task-deficient regime (<math id="S2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), where it does not.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Decomposition of ICL Prediction</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our high-dimensional analysis reveals how structured tasks are learned in context.
The model’s prediction decomposes into a core algorithmic signal and two noise terms that can be suppressed by context.
This view reinterprets ICL as a context-dependent noise-reduction mechanism, providing a framework for how general-purpose models adapt their computation.
Formally, the prediction is given by the following result:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_result">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Decomposition of ICL prediction)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The prediction <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for a given prompt is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\operatorname{tr}(W^{*}H^{\top})\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\hat{y}_{\mathrm{algo}}+\hat{y}_{\mathrm{mem}}+\hat{y}_{\mathrm{struct}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">d</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>algo</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>mem</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>struct</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\operatorname{tr}(W^{*}H^{\top})\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\hat{y}_{\mathrm{algo}}+\hat{y}_{\mathrm{mem}}+\hat{y}_{\mathrm{struct}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">d</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}</annotation></semantics></math> represents equality in distribution.
This decomposition yields a deterministic <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">algorithmic signal</span> (<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\mathrm{algo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>algo</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\mathrm{algo}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and two independent noise terms: a <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">memorization noise</span> (<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\mathrm{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>mem</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\mathrm{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math>) correlated with the pre-training tasks, and a <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">structure noise</span> (<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\mathrm{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>struct</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\mathrm{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math>) uncorrelated with them. This decomposition holds for any pre-training task distribution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">3</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">We use a replica method </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="footnote3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mézard et al., 1987</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Charbonneau et al., 2023</a><span id="footnote3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite><span id="footnote3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> in statistical physics to analyze the asymptotic limit. This method involves a non-rigorous mathematical step. Consequently, we conservatively state our findings as “Results” rather than “Theorems”. However, the validity of our theoretical predictions is confirmed by numerical experiments, which show excellent agreement (see Appendix </span><a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Consistency of the Replica Method with Numerical Experiments" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a><span id="footnote3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">).</span></span></span></span>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The next result characterizes each component analytically for <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\gg 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\gg 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_result">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic Characterization of the Components)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume data-rich and task-rich regimes. Each term in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In Result 3.1 (Decomposition of ICL prediction). ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{algo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>algo</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{algo}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\ab(P_{A}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}})^{\top}\mathbf{x}+\mathcal{O}\ab(\frac{1}{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">d</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>prompt</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\ab(P_{A}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}})^{\top}\mathbf{x}+\mathcal{O}\ab(\frac{1}{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>mem</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\frac{\sigma\sqrt{\rho}}{\gamma-1/\kappa}Z_{\mathrm{mem}}+\mathcal{O}\ab(\frac{1}{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">d</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>mem</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\frac{\sigma\sqrt{\rho}}{\gamma-1/\kappa}Z_{\mathrm{mem}}+\mathcal{O}\ab(\frac{1}{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>struct</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\frac{\sigma\rho}{\sqrt{\ab(1+\sigma^{2})\ab(\gamma-1/\kappa)}}Z_{\mathrm{struct}}\sqrt{\alpha}+\mathcal{O}\ab(\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">d</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>struct</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mi>α</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\frac{\sigma\rho}{\sqrt{\ab(1+\sigma^{2})\ab(\gamma-1/\kappa)}}Z_{\mathrm{struct}}\sqrt{\alpha}+\mathcal{O}\ab(\frac{1}{\sqrt{\alpha}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where random variables <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathrm{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>mem</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathrm{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\mathrm{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>struct</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\mathrm{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\mathrm{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>mem</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\mathrm{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\mathcal{N}\ab(0,\ab(1/M_{0})\ab(A^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}})^{\top}S_{v}^{-1}\ab(A^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>prompt</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>prompt</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\mathcal{N}\ab(0,\ab(1/M_{0})\ab(A^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}})^{\top}S_{v}^{-1}\ab(A^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{\mathrm{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>struct</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{\mathrm{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\mathcal{N}\ab(0,\ab(1/M_{0})\absolutevalue{P_A^{\perp} \vb{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}}}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>prompt</mi></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\mathcal{N}\ab(0,\ab(1/M_{0})\absolutevalue{P_A^{\perp} \vb{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}}}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">respectively. Here, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{v}=(1/M_{0})\sum_{\mu}\mathbf{v}^{\mu}{\mathbf{v}^{\mu}}^{\top},P_{A}=AA^{\top},P_{A}^{\perp}=I_{D}-P_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>μ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{v}=(1/M_{0})\sum_{\mu}\mathbf{v}^{\mu}{\mathbf{v}^{\mu}}^{\top},P_{A}=AA^{\top},P_{A}^{\perp}=I_{D}-P_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}}=1/\tilde{\alpha}\sum_{l}y_{l}\mathbf{x}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>prompt</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\mathrm{prompt}}=1/\tilde{\alpha}\sum_{l}y_{l}\mathbf{x}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the formal statement and derivation of Results. <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Result 3.1 (Decomposition of ICL prediction). ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Result 3.2 (Asymptotic Characterization of the Components). ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Replica Calculation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a clear interpretation of this result,
we plot the contribution of each component to the generalization error, i.e., <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{protocol}}=\mathbb{E}[(y-\hat{y}_{\text{algo}})^{2}]+\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}_{\text{mem}}^{2}]+\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}_{\text{struct}}^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>protocol</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>algo</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mem</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>struct</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{protocol}}=\mathbb{E}[(y-\hat{y}_{\text{algo}})^{2}]+\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}_{\text{mem}}^{2}]+\mathbb{E}[\hat{y}_{\text{struct}}^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title='Figure 1 ‣ The Suppressible Noise Terms (𝑦̂_"mem",𝑦̂_"struct"): ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Algorithmic Signal (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\text{algo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>algo</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\text{algo}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The leading term, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\text{algo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>algo</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\text{algo}}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents the model’s core algorithmic competency.
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In Result 3.2 (Asymptotic Characterization of the Components). ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) reveals that ICL can acquire a sophisticated two-step procedure learned implicitly from pre-training without any explicit regularization.
First, the model computes a naive (Matched Filter) estimator <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}=1/\tilde{\alpha}\sum_{l}y_{l}\mathbf{x}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}=1/\tilde{\alpha}\sum_{l}y_{l}\mathbf{x}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> from the prompt examples.
Second, it projects this estimate onto the low-rank subspace defined by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{A}=AA^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{A}=AA^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents the structural prior learned from the pre-training task distribution.
This term is the primary signal of the prediction.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title='Figure 1 ‣ The Suppressible Noise Terms (𝑦̂_"mem",𝑦̂_"struct"): ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the error contribution from this algorithmic component consistently decreases as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases, indicating that a long context improves the model’s algorithmic accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The Suppressible Noise Terms (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\text{mem}},\hat{y}_{\text{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mem</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>struct</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\text{mem}},\hat{y}_{\text{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis shows that the model’s versatility across tasks inherently introduces noise, and that ICL operates by selectively suppressing these noise terms through context. This dual effect explains how ICL supports general-purpose adaptability, and the following two terms illustrate how it manifests in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\text{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>mem</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\text{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a memory-dependent noise.
Eq. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Result 3.2 (Asymptotic Characterization of the Components). ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) shows that its magnitude is determined by the squared Mahalanobis distance between the projected task estimate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\top}\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the memorized task matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> in the latent space.
This distance becomes small when the current task estimate is statistically typical of the memorized tasks, i.e., it aligns with directions in which the tasks <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{v}^{\mu}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{v}^{\mu}\}</annotation></semantics></math> have high variance.
As seen in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title='Figure 1 ‣ The Suppressible Noise Terms (𝑦̂_"mem",𝑦̂_"struct"): ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this memorization noise is lower for TM tasks compared to unfamiliar IDG tasks, as the model can successfully recall the familiar task.
However, this term reveals a trade-off with respect to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.
While a longer context reduces algorithmic and structural errors, it paradoxically worsens this noise, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title='Figure 1 ‣ The Suppressible Noise Terms (𝑦̂_"mem",𝑦̂_"struct"): ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in the IDG protocol.
This is a consequence of task-overfitting: with large <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the model’s over-specialization to the specific patterns of pre-training instances leads to poorer generalization on new tasks, thereby increasing the memorization error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The term <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\text{struct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>struct</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\text{struct}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a structure-dependent noise.
The estimator <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{A}^{\perp}\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{A}^{\perp}\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Result 3.2 (Asymptotic Characterization of the Components). ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) explicitly measures the component of the in-context estimate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is orthogonal to the learned structure <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
This term can be interpreted as an error signal generated by the portion of the prompt that violates the learned structural prior.
Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title='Figure 1 ‣ The Suppressible Noise Terms (𝑦̂_"mem",𝑦̂_"struct"): ‣ 3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> clearly shows this: for ODG tasks, the fundamental structural mismatch causes this noise component to become pronounced and dominate the error.
Conversely, for in-distribution tasks (TM and IDG), a longer context allows the model to better identify the underlying structure, leading to a decrease in this structural noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our decomposition shows that ICL predictions consist of an algorithmic signal and two suppressible noise terms, whose interplay explains the model’s adaptability across diverse tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04548v1/decomposed_error.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/169;" width="673" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Decomposition of generalization error reveals ICL’s noise-reduction mechanism.</span>
The total generalization error (in theory with dashed lines, and in numerical experiments with error bars)
and its decomposition into algorithmic (signal), memorization (noise), and structural (noise) components (solid lines) for each protocol.
Parameters: <math id="S3.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1.5,\kappa=1.5,\rho=0.9,\sigma=0.3,\tilde{\alpha}=\alpha,D=60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1.5,\kappa=1.5,\rho=0.9,\sigma=0.3,\tilde{\alpha}=\alpha,D=60</annotation></semantics></math>.
The error bars represent the standard errors of the mean over <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> independent numerical experiments per point.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Decomposition of ICL Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> showed that the model learns a low-rank regression algorithm; here, we investigate how this learning remains stable.
This is particularly puzzling in low-rank settings (<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>) where, despite the absence of explicit regularization in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Pretraining Phase ‣ 2 Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), our model achieves robust generalization.
To understand this mechanism, we first examine the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">idealized limit</span> of infinite pre-training context length (<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> before learning).
Contrary to the intuition that perfect data should improve performance, we demonstrate that this limit, lacking finite-sample fluctuations, renders the learning problem ill-posed.
This leads to an initialization-sensitive solution and poor generalization, a stark contrast to the stable learning in the <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">asymptotic limit</span> (<math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> after learning).
Our theoretical explanation attributes this stability to an implicit regularization induced by finite-data statistics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Instability in the Idealized Limit</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a numerical experiment to compare the model’s IDG error under two conditions: a standard setting with a finite number of demonstrations <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and an idealized limit.
The latter is implemented by training on the expected feature matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\text{ideal}}^{\mu}=\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}[H^{\mu}]=\mathbf{x}_{L+1}^{\mu}{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mtext>ideal</mtext><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\text{ideal}}^{\mu}=\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}[H^{\mu}]=\mathbf{x}_{L+1}^{\mu}{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In both cases, the model is trained via gradient descent from a random initialization <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{w}_{0}\|=D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{w}_{0}\|=D</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting IDG error is plotted as a function of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Instability in the Idealized Limit ‣ 4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The plot shows that for any <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the error in the idealized limit fails to decrease,
whereas for finite <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the error systematically improves as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
This result is counter-intuitive; the idealized model, which is given perfect information about the ground-truth task <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\text{ideal}}^{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mtext>ideal</mtext><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\text{ideal}}^{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math>, was expected to perform better,
yet it is outperformed by the model that must infer the task from finite demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04548v1/implicit_reg_experiment_GD.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:337/169;" width="337" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implicit regularization from finite data prevents learning instability.</span>
IDG error as a function of the task subspace dimensionality <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
The figure compares the performance of the idealized limit (dashed line), with standard setting using finite sample ratio <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (solid lines).
Parameters: <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=40,M_{0}=60,\tilde{L}=160,M=2400,\sigma=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>160</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2400</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=40,M_{0}=60,\tilde{L}=160,M=2400,\sigma=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>.
The error bars represent the standard errors of the mean over <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> trials per point.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Theoretical Explanation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To explain this phenomenon, our theoretical analysis reveals that the learned weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> behaves as if it were the solution to an effective optimization problem, which differs critically depending on whether the idealized limit or the asymptotic limit is considered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_result">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Effective Objective Function)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist scalars <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, determined solely by the system parameters, such that the statistics of the learned weight matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalent to those of the solution to the following optimization problem, i.e.,
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W^{*}\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\operatorname*{argmin}f(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">d</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmin</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{*}\overset{\mathrm{d}}{=}\operatorname*{argmin}f(W)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the effective objective function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(W)=\tr\ab[\frac{\hat{q}}{2}WSW^{\top}-\mathcal{M}_{S}W^{\top}+\frac{\hat{\bar{q}}}{2}WW^{\top}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext class="undefined">\ab</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(W)=\tr\ab[\frac{\hat{q}}{2}WSW^{\top}-\mathcal{M}_{S}W^{\top}+\frac{\hat{\bar{q}}}{2}WW^{\top}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, The matrix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(1/M_{0})\sum_{\mu=1}^{M_{0}}\mathbf{w}^{\mu}{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(1/M_{0})\sum_{\mu=1}^{M_{0}}\mathbf{w}^{\mu}{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is the task covariance matrix, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is a random noise matrix whose structure depends on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_result">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotics of Regularization)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the scalars in the effective objective function (Result <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Result 4.1 (Effective Objective Function). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical Explanation ‣ 4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) scale as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathcal{M}_{S}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathcal{M}_{S}\|=\mathcal{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The effective regularization coefficient <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{O}(1/\alpha)&gt;0&amp;(\text{in the asymptotic limit})\\
0&amp;(\text{in the idealized limit}).\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">in the asymptotic limit</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">in the idealized limit</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{O}(1/\alpha)&gt;0&amp;(\text{in the asymptotic limit})\\
0&amp;(\text{in the idealized limit}).\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the formal statement and derivation, see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Replica Calculation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These results provide a clear explanation for the observed phenomenon.
The term <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}\operatorname{tr}(WW^{\top})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}\operatorname{tr}(WW^{\top})/2</annotation></semantics></math> acts as an effective <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization on the weight matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Result <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Result 4.2 (Asymptotics of Regularization). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical Explanation ‣ 4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> reveals that this crucial term emerges only when the pre-training dataset is finite (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>).
This regularization is essential for learning low-rank tasks (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>), where the task covariance matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is rank-deficient.
Without it (i.e., when <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in the idealized limit), the objective function possesses flat directions corresponding to the null space of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the minimization problem becomes ill-posed and lacks a unique minimizer, leading to the learning instability seen in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Instability in the Idealized Limit ‣ 4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, this framework explains why performance improves as <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> increases.
The effective regularization strength, given by the ratio <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bar{q}}/\hat{q}\sim\mathcal{O}(1/\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bar{q}}/\hat{q}\sim\mathcal{O}(1/\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, is naturally annealed as more data becomes available.
This allows the model to rely more on the data and converge to a more accurate solution while avoiding instability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Implications</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This finding has several significant implications for theory and practice.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ICL as a Two-step Automatic Learning Process.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We hypothesize that the origin of this implicit regularization lies in the shared structure and the two-stage process inherent to ICL.
We can think of the first stage as task identification, where the model identifies the task from the pre-training dataset.
The statistical fluctuations in this data ensure the resulting internal task representation is never perfectly low-rank and always contains a small, full-rank noise component.
This unavoidable noise then becomes a beneficial regularizer during the second stage, which we can call inference execution.
It breaks the flat directions of the optimization landscape that would otherwise cause learning instability in the idealized limit.
In this way, the statistical imperfection in identifying the task is precisely what provides the stability for its execution in low-rank tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Non-Commutative Finite-Sample Effect.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our result demonstrates that the operations of pre-training and taking the infinite-data limit are non-commutative:</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Pretrain}\left(\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\mathsf{Data}\right)\neq\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\mathsf{Pretrain}\left(\mathsf{Data}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝗋𝖾𝗍𝗋𝖺𝗂𝗇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mi>𝖣𝖺𝗍𝖺</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≠</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝖯𝗋𝖾𝗍𝗋𝖺𝗂𝗇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝖣𝖺𝗍𝖺</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Pretrain}\left(\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\mathsf{Data}\right)\neq\lim_{\alpha\to\infty}\mathsf{Pretrain}\left(\mathsf{Data}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">While traditional statistical learning theory often relies on asymptotic analyses where finite-sample effects are secondary, our work shows that the statistical fluctuations inherent in finite data can play a primary and essential role in ensuring stable and effective learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data as a Regularizer.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In deep learning, implicit regularization is often attributed to optimization algorithms like SGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2017</a>]</cite>.
While Transformers are also known to exhibit implicit regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vasudeva et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frei and Vardi, 2025</a>]</cite>,
existing theories often treat it as an extension of standard supervised learning.
In contrast, our findings reveal a distinct form of regularization originating from the statistical fluctuations of a finite dataset. We attribute this phenomenon to the unique ICL objective of automatically acquiring an algorithm.
This implies that for certain structures, learning is ironically hindered by idealized, noise-free data, suggesting that the natural statistical noise in a dataset can be a crucial feature, not a bug to be removed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Trade-offs from Task Difficulty</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on our finding that the model learns a stable low-rank regression algorithm, we now explore how the model’s capability is governed by the properties of this low-rank structure.
We develop a theoretical framework based on eigenvalue spectral analysis, which predicts a transition in the model’s capabilities. This prediction is later validated by our results, revealing a sharp phase transition and fundamental trade-off between specialization and out-of-distribution robustness.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Spectral Analysis of Task Structure</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the model’s behavior, we draw an analogy to classical linear regression, where the Gram matrix is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=X^{\top}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=X^{\top}X</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the eigenbasis of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the variance of the estimated coefficient along the direction associated with eigenvalue <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Var}(\hat{w}_{i})\propto\sigma^{2}/\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖵𝖺𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Var}(\hat{w}_{i})\propto\sigma^{2}/\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise variance.
A large eigenvalue of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a feature direction with a high signal-to-noise ratio, leading to statistical stability.
A small eigenvalue indicates an ill-conditioned direction that is highly sensitive to noise.
Thus, the number and magnitude of nonzero eigenvalues jointly determine the model’s learnable patterns and their stability.
A zero eigenvalue means that no learnable pattern exists in the corresponding direction, so any apparent estimate comes only from regularization or noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Applying this analogy to our ICL framework, we can explain the model’s capabilities by analyzing the eigenvalue distribution of the task covariance matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=(1/M_{0})\sum_{\mu=1}^{M_{0}}\mathbf{w}^{\mu}{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=(1/M_{0})\sum_{\mu=1}^{M_{0}}\mathbf{w}^{\mu}{\mathbf{w}^{\mu}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Result 4.1 (Effective Objective Function). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical Explanation ‣ 4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), which encapsulates the task information learned during pre-training.
This follows from the fact that, in the eigenbasis of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> with eigenvalues <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the variance of the corresponding estimator scales as <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Var}(W_{ij})\propto 1/(\hat{q}s_{j}+\hat{\bar{q}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖵𝖺𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Var}(W_{ij})\propto 1/(\hat{q}s_{j}+\hat{\bar{q}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In Result 4.1 (Effective Objective Function). ‣ 4.2 Theoretical Explanation ‣ 4 Finite Pre-training Data Induces Implicit Regularization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).
The following theorem precisely characterizes this distribution of eigenvalues and predicts that its structure changes at the transition point <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Spectrum of the Task Matrix)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=r/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=r/D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=M_{0}/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=M_{0}/D</annotation></semantics></math>. The empirical spectral distribution of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> converges almost surely to</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{S}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{S}(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(1-\min(\rho,\kappa)\right)\delta(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(1-\min(\rho,\kappa)\right)\delta(s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathbf{1}_{[s_{-},s_{+}]}(s)\,\frac{\rho\kappa}{2\pi s}\sqrt{(s_{+}-s)(s-s_{-})}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathbf{1}_{[s_{-},s_{+}]}(s)\,\frac{\rho\kappa}{2\pi s}\sqrt{(s_{+}-s)(s-s_{-})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{[s_{-},s_{+}]}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{[s_{-},s_{+}]}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. The support edges are given by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\pm}=\left(\sqrt{\rho}\pm\sqrt{\kappa}\right)^{2}/(\rho\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>ρ</mi></msqrt><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\pm}=\left(\sqrt{\rho}\pm\sqrt{\kappa}\right)^{2}/(\rho\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is given in Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Proof of Theorem" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem provides a quantitative basis for predicting the model’s performance by revealing how the learning bottleneck shifts.
In the task-deficient regime (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), the number of learnable patterns is limited by <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> (the fraction of nonzero eigenvalues).
Moreover, the typical eigenvalue magnitude scales as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that the statistical stability of these patterns is also controlled by <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a result, the model’s capabilities do not change even as tasks get simpler, indicating the model’s learning is bottlenecked by task diversity.
In contrast, in the task-rich regime (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), the number of nonzero eigenvalues is <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, and their magnitudes scale as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As tasks become simpler, the larger eigenvalues concentrate useful information, allowing the model to fully exploit the simplified low-rank structure for more stable and efficient learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Phase Transition in ICL Capabilities</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To connect our spectral analysis with practical ICL performance, we derive the theoretical generalization errors for three settings: TM, IDG, and ODG.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_result">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Result 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The generalization errors <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>IDG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{ODG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>ODG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{ODG}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be analytically obtained by solving a finite system of self-consistent equations.
In particular, when the asymptotic case <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\gamma&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\gamma&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0</annotation></semantics></math>, each metric is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\min(\rho,\kappa)}{\tilde{\alpha}}\left(1+\frac{1-\min(\rho,\kappa)}{\kappa\gamma-1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\min(\rho,\kappa)}{\tilde{\alpha}}\left(1+\frac{1-\min(\rho,\kappa)}{\kappa\gamma-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>IDG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}&amp;\text{for }\rho&lt;\kappa\\
1-\frac{\kappa}{\rho}-\frac{\kappa(\kappa-\rho)}{\rho(\tau-1)}+\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}&amp;\text{for }\rho\geq\kappa\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}&amp;\text{for }\rho&lt;\kappa\\
1-\frac{\kappa}{\rho}-\frac{\kappa(\kappa-\rho)}{\rho(\tau-1)}+\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}&amp;\text{for }\rho\geq\kappa\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{ODG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>ODG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{ODG}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-2\min(\rho,\kappa)+(1+\tilde{\alpha})\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-2\min(\rho,\kappa)+(1+\tilde{\alpha})\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the formal statement and derivation, see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Replica Calculation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Result <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Result 5.2. ‣ 5.2 Phase Transition in ICL Capabilities ‣ 5 Trade-offs from Task Difficulty" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, these errors exhibit a sharp phase transition at the predicted critical point <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2 Phase Transition in ICL Capabilities ‣ 5 Trade-offs from Task Difficulty" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates this behavior by showing how the generalization errors change as <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> varies with fixed <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>: for finite <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> we observe a crossover, which sharpens into a distinct phase transition as <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, dividing the generalization behavior into two regimes.
In the task-deficient regime (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), both TM and ODG errors remain on a plateau, consistent with our spectral analysis.
In this regime, the model’s performance is unaffected by task difficulty, since learning is bottlenecked by task diversity.
In the task-rich regime (<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), as task difficulty decreases, the ODG error increases, while the IDG and TM errors decrease in tandem and remain identical.
This outcome demonstrates that the model can perfectly capture the in-distribution task structure when <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, as reflected in the alignment of IDG and TM errors.
It also shows that strong specialization to the low-rank task structure enhances memorization and in-distribution performance, but at the expense of out-of-distribution robustness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we analyze the role of <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Result <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Result 5.2. ‣ 5.2 Phase Transition in ICL Capabilities ‣ 5 Trade-offs from Task Difficulty" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> shows that in the task-rich regime the <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>IDG</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}</annotation></semantics></math> scale as <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\tilde{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\tilde{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This means the model has learned the in-distribution task structure, but finite <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> limits the retrieval of this structure from the prompt.
In the limit <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the errors vanish as <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>TM</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>IDG</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{TM}}=\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{IDG}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that the model acquires the ability to achieve complete in-distribution generalization once given enough prompt examples, with the transition occurring at <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\kappa</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            The sudden acquisition of this IDG ability may represent a novel type of <span id="footnote4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">emergence</span>. Unlike standard emergent phenomena in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raventós et al., 2023</a>]</cite>, which are typically driven by increasing dataset size or model scale, this transition is induced by a change in the intrinsic structure of the task distribution itself, as predicted by our theory.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04548v1/plateau.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:303/202;" width="303" height="202"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S5.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Phase transition in the model’s capabilities.</span>
Generalization errors (<math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{TM}},\mathcal{E}_{\text{IDG}},\mathcal{E}_{\text{ODG}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>TM</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>IDG</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>ODG</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{TM}},\mathcal{E}_{\text{IDG}},\mathcal{E}_{\text{ODG}}</annotation></semantics></math>) as a function of the task difficulty <math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
The results confirm the predicted phase transition in generalization errors at <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.
The error bars represent the standard errors of the mean over <math id="S5.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> trials per point.
Parameters: <math id="S5.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=200\text{ (solid lines)},\alpha\to\infty\text{ (dashed lines)},\tilde{\alpha}=4.0,\gamma=8.0,\kappa=0.5,\sigma=0,D=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>200</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> (solid lines)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> (dashed lines)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8.0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=200\text{ (solid lines)},\alpha\to\infty\text{ (dashed lines)},\tilde{\alpha}=4.0,\gamma=8.0,\kappa=0.5,\sigma=0,D=40</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Implications for Pre-training Strategy</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These theoretically grounded findings reveal that the balance between task difficulty and diversity is a critical lever for tailoring a model’s capabilities. This provides a clear framework for pre-training strategy based on desired downstream performance.
For instance, developing a specialized model for a specific domain mandates operating in the task-rich regime. This approach, which involves curating a dataset with high diversity of relatively simple tasks, enables the model to efficiently master domain-specific patterns and achieve high TM and IDG performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For many practical applications, however, the most prudent and efficient strategy is to target the “sweet spot” near the phase transition (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>). This point represents an optimal trade-off and secures robust ODG performance. It also proves most resource-efficient, without the need to prepare unnecessarily complex training data based on difficult tasks.
Thus, the balance between task difficulty and diversity is a core design choice that shapes the model’s capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, our findings results are consistent with the intuition of curriculum learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al., 2009</a>]</cite>, which introduces simple tasks first and gradually shifts to more complex ones. In our framework, the early stage of training corresponds to low task diversity (<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>), where the model cannot yet learn complex tasks and thus only benefits from simpler ones. As training progresses and task diversity increases, the model enters a regime where it can exploit the richer structure to master more complex and abstract tasks. This explains why a curriculum that progresses from simple to difficult tasks is effective.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our study sheds light on the mechanisms of in-context learning (ICL) in linear transformers.
We show that ICL functions as a context-dependent noise-reduction mechanism, enabling the model to perform a precise low-rank regression algorithm when the task structure is aligned with the context.
We further demonstrate that statistical fluctuations in finite training data give rise to an implicit regularization, which stabilizes the learning process.
Finally, we uncover a sharp phase transition governed by task structure, highlighting a fundamental trade-off between specialization and robustness under distributional shifts.
These results together provide a coherent framework for understanding how transformers learn to learn.</p>
</div>
</section>
